The Go rendering: two modules, the target and the dependency its module file
requires and reaches through a replace naming the dependency directory relative to
the target. The target imports a package the dependency provides, so the
requirement is backed and the replace is applied, and neither directive is
reported. The replace is what makes the requirement resolvable, so the rendering
loads with no network access and the required module is never fetched.

-- fixture.json --
{
  "symbols": {
    "AppliedReplace": { "file": "target/go.mod", "line": 7 },
    "BackedRequirement": { "file": "target/go.mod", "line": 5 }
  }
}
-- target/go.mod --
module example.com/target

go 1.27.0

require example.com/dep v0.0.0

replace example.com/dep v0.0.0 => ../dep
-- target/main.go --
// Command target imports the module its own module file requires, so the
// requirement is backed by an import and the replace that resolves it is applied.
package main

import "example.com/dep"

func main() {
	println(dep.Value())
}
-- dep/go.mod --
module example.com/dep

go 1.27.0
-- dep/value.go --
// Package dep is the second module of the rendering: the target's module file
// requires it and names this directory through a replace.
package dep

// Value is the declaration the target references.
func Value() int { return 1 }
